{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life involved complex political maneuvers and military conflicts that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and this position placed him at the center of court factions fighting for influence over the young king. Raymond often clashed with other nobles such as Guy of Lusignan and these internal divisions weakened the Christian defenses against Saladin.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond inherited his title after his father Raymond II was assassinated by the Order of Assassins. He was only a child when he became count so guardians managed his lands until he reached maturity. During his youth he spent time at the Byzantine court where he learned Greek customs and developed diplomatic skills that would serve him well later. His return to Tripoli marked the beginning of a long tenure filled with both peace treaties and violent skirmishes against Muslim neighbors. He married Eschiva of Bures which brought him additional lands and strengthened his political standing within the kingdom.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe period when Raymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV was fraught with tension between different noble families. He supported the idea of making alliances with Muslim powers to ensure survival while others preferred aggressive warfare. This difference in strategy caused deep rifts within the royal court and led to several failed campaigns against Saladin. Raymond opposed the marriage of Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan because he feared Guy lacked the wisdom to rule effectively. His opposition made him many enemies among the higher nobility who viewed his caution as cowardice or treason.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nThe disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 resulted in the near total destruction of the Crusader army and Raymond was one of the few commanders who escaped capture. Some contemporaries accused him of betraying the Christian forces by withdrawing his troops early in the fight although modern historians debate this claim. He died shortly after the battle possibly from illness or despair over the loss of Jerusalem to Saladin. His legacy remains controversial since some see him as a pragmatic leader trying to save what he could while others view him as a traitor who contributed to the collapse of the kingdom. The county of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond Roupen but stability remained elusive for years afterward.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.4836423e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0020673173}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.009602188}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0067216246}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -9.298757e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.3113031e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.264979e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0008883248}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.21809734}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.06490359}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logp